Java has exactly 8 primitive data types. They are split into categories:

**Integer types**: `byte` (8-bit, -128 to 127), `short` (16-bit), `int` (32-bit, most common), `long` (64-bit, use `L` suffix like `100L`)

**Floating-point types**: `float` (32-bit, use `f` suffix like `3.14f`), `double` (64-bit, default for decimals)

**Other**: `char` (16-bit Unicode character, e.g. `'A'`), `boolean` (true/false)

```java
byte b = 100;
short s = 30000;
int i = 100000;
long l = 10000000000L;
float f = 3.14f;
double d = 3.14159;
char c = 'A';
boolean flag = true;
```

```java
public class Employee {
    static int companyCount = 0;   // static variable — shared by ALL instances
    String name;                    // instance variable — each Employee has own name
    int salary;                     // instance variable

    void giveRaise() {
        int bonus = 5000;           // local variable — only alive inside this method
        salary += bonus;
    }
}
```

**Instance variables** are declared in the class body (not in a method). They're created when you do `new Employee()` and destroyed when the object is GC'd. They get automatic defaults.

**Local variables** live only for the duration of a method call. The compiler forces you to initialize them before use — there's no automatic default.

**Static variables** are class-level. Only one copy exists regardless of how many objects you create.
